WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.879
Is the world getting better or is

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.009
it on the verge of collapse?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.229
Stefan Sagmeister emphatically

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.969
believes that things are looking up

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.859
and in the art exhibition "Now

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.929
Is Better" showcases a bold

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.429
new way to convince the world that

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.509
he's right.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.309
He takes actual paintings from the

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.349
18th and 19th centuries,

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.329
disassembles them and creates

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.159
new works by juxtaposing them

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.049
with data visualizations

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.609
of just how much things have

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.129
improved since the good old days.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.479
Some works chart the incredible

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.039
decline over the centuries and

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.839
deaths on the battlefield from

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.729
famine and from extreme weather

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.679
events, while others show how

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.539
much cheaper food and lighting have

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.369
become in real terms.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.499
One piece documents the explosion

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.209
in the number of guitars per person

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.099
on the planet, an indicator of

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.289
growth in leisure and entertainment,

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.299
while another charts the persistent

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.459
belief that crime is always rising

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.799
despite its well-documented decline.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.689
A heralded graphic designer who has

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.969
designed album covers for Jay-Z,

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.460
The Rolling Stones, and Lou Reed.

00:01:04.461 --> 00:01:06.359
He's won two Grammy

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.159
Awards, including one for his design

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.079
of the Talking Heads boxset

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.969
"Once In a Lifetime."

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.859
Born in Austria in 1962,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.719
he's called New York City home since

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.759
the 1990s, and he draws

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.439
on sources such as "Our World in

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.269
Data," Human Progress

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.189
and the work of Steven Pinker,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.629
who has written the foreword to an

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.064
art book of the "Now Is Better"

00:01:26.065 --> 00:01:28.019
series that's due out later

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.999
this year in a wide ranging

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.989
conversation.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.029
Sagmeister tells me why it's so

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.189
important to acknowledge and defend

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.539
material progress.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.239
Why art and commerce are not enemies

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.859
and what he loves about the new

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.079
world he's adopted as his homeland

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.059
and how that ties in to the "Now

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.170
is Better" project.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.929
Stefan Sagmeister, Thanks for

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:53.889
talking to Reason.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:54.759
Wonderful.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:55.809
Thank you so much.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.699
So give me the elevator pitch on the

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.019
"Now Is Better" show.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.109
Well, it's basically comes

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.209
out of my interest

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.159
in long term thinking

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.189
because I discovered

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.229
that there are really two ways

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:12.729
to look at the world.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.409
One is from a very short term

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.249
perspective.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.169
What is happening right now, which

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.209
is basically what all media

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.409
looks at it.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.359
And if you do that,

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.879
almost by definition,

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.799
everything looks bad

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.719
because things that

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.879
are going bad go bad

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.199
quickly.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.089
Scandals, catastrophes and

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.399
so on. While many things that

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.229
develop well that develop

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.359
positively, do so very slowly

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.279
and don't really work

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.289
in our short news cycle.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.269
But that's

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:48.489
really what the show is about,

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.469
to look at the world from a

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.929
long term perspective.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.939
20 years, 50 years, 200

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.229
years. I think we have a couple of

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.149
pieces that are 500 years

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.129
and there, if you

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:01.959
take that point of view,

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.699
many, many things look very

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:04.239
positive.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.219
Was there a moment where

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.529
you were like, Aha, this is

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.179
this is what my work is going to be

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.012
for the next few years?

00:03:12.013 --> 00:03:13.013
There was. Well,

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.479
I was lucky enough to get one

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.729
of those residencies at the American

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:20.169
Academy in Rome.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.149
And one evening I was sitting

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.159
next to a lawyer

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.809
who was the husband of one of the

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.759
residents artists, and he

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.729
told me that what we see in

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.619
Poland, in Hungary

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.539
and Brazil at the time

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.369
really means the end of modern

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.649
democracy. He was a highly educated

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.719
person, and I looked it up.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.669
I went back to the studio that

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.499
night and quickly Googled

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.329
it. What's the story of this modern

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.579
democracy? When did it start?

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.439
And if you went back

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.449
200 years, there really was only

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.189
one in the United States.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.019
I went back a hundred years

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.540
after World War I.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.479
There were, I think, 18.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.369
And now we have just

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.889
a good 80, I think 87

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.569
democracies that the U.N.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.849
says they are democracies.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.009
So it turned out that this

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.289
smart man

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.299
had really no idea of the road

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.069
that he lives in. But I discussed it

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:19.869
with other people and was

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.699
immediately surprised

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.369
how much pushback I would get

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.219
because, you know, specifically

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.749
there were certain people and in

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:29.979
this case it was mostly on the

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.229
political left who somehow

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.149
want to believe that things

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.129
are going down, that there

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:37.689
is some sort of an investment in

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:37.939
that.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.219
What do you think that is?

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.869
I think if you're an activist, it

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.119
might be easier to get people

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:44.949
over to your cause if you can

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.869
tell them how bad things are.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.969
And I think there's a truth to that.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.189
Many of us being so

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.289
overwhelmed by all the negative

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.749
news that we get on a daily basis,

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:57.579
give up and say, Oh, this is so

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.799
terrible, we can do anything anyway.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.809
One of the paintings that

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:03.699
you have is titled "Richer and

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.159
Poorer," and it shows

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:07.160
in 2020, 9%

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:09.699
of the global population was living

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.189
in poverty compared to in 1990.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.019
35% of

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.519
the global population was living in

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:16.419
poverty.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.279
But then you ask how

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.749
many people believe poverty has

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.419
gotten worse and 55%

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.339
of people believe that poverty

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.959
has gotten worse over the past 30

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.149
years. Only 12%

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.349
believe that it's gotten better,

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:30.249
which it has.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.619
So are people misinformed

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.419
or do they want to believe that

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.759
they're living in a bad period?

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:37.989
I think it's a mix.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.969
I think it's a it's

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:41.889
a mix of I don't believe that

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.479
the media is.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.149
Inherently evil or.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.089
Or is trying to mislead people?

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:49.539
No,

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:50.979
They believe what they're saying.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.239
They believe what they're saying.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.609
And they know specifically

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:56.589
now in the last 20

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:58.839
years since we have clickable

00:05:58.840 --> 00:05:59.769
links.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:02.139
They know what people like.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.179
The media gives us what

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.939
we like, and we all

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.189
prefer negative news over positive

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:11.079
news. I am just

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.249
like everybody else.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:14.559
I also do prefer

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.419
negativity and I

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:19.089
actually think to create

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.159
pieces that are positive

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.529
is somewhat more challenging

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:25.629
because they by

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.459
themselves tend to

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:29.169
be less interesting.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.449
The vast majority

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.729
of pieces, when they have anything

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.889
to do with

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:37.929
commentary of life,

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.819
social commentary, anything

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.339
will be critical or negative.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.319
One of the series of pieces

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.419
in the collection look at how

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:47.949
much richer we are.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.199
One actually looks at global GDP

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:51.778
and how much richer the planet is

00:06:51.779 --> 00:06:52.779
which is fascinating.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.239
One painting is "Bright Lights:

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.579
Time worked for an hour of light,"

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.529
and in 1800 there

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:01.479
were 6 hours of work for an hour of

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.679
light. In 2000, it was 2

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.589
seconds, work for an hour of light.

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.629
You have another one about 3 hours

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:08.619
working a job.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.809
And it's amazing that

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:12.669
not only has the amount of work you

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.559
have to do for a second of

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.479
light has come down,

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:18.369
but the amount that we work

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:19.299
is much less.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.459
How do people respond when you say,

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:23.409
"Well, actually you're working a lot

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:25.629
less than your your father probably,

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:26.630
or your grandparents?"

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:29.589
On the work front, I

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.529
think there

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.519
was an interesting phenomena

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.499
going on right now in

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:37.329
France, you know, where you have all

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:38.920
these protests going on

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:40.940
against moving

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.219
the pension years up,

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.619
and I think that's a direct

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.539
result of

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:51.759
this information not being

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.919
part of our daily

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.059
discussion.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:57.099
Because if everybody would

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.989
be aware that we

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:01.239
now live double

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:03.579
the amount of time than we did

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.499
100 years ago, then it's just

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:07.599
logical that we would have

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.639
to work a little bit more before

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.529
we can go into

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:13.179
our retirement.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.039
You have a piece called "Artists,

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:16.929
Lawyers and Doctors" and this is

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:18.249
the number of artists, lawyers and

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:19.779
doctors in the U.S.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.149
Artists 2.5 million.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.039
Lawyers, 1.3 million.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.619
Doctors 900,000.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:28.389
Is it a sign of a good society that

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:30.519
there are more artists than doctors?

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:32.469
It's definitely the sign of a good

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:34.449
society that it would

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:36.489
be able to sustain

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:39.038
a very large group of people

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:41.349
that has very little function.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.569
We can't do anything before

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:45.699
we'd have shelter, before we can't

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.559
breathe and eat.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.389
And so all of these

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.429
other things, and specifically

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:54.579
the artistic impetus,

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.629
is only be able to even

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:58.449
to be pursued once

00:08:58.450 --> 00:08:59.499
we have shelter.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.059
And once we have

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:02.979
The cave paintings are of the

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:04.949
animals that hunters caught

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:05.949
so

00:09:04.951 --> 00:09:05.049
Exactly

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:06.999
you can paint

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.649
after you've eaten, but probably not

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:09.189
before.

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:11.139
And they're done in a cave once you

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:12.029
are sheltered.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:12.499
Right. Right.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:12.849
Yeah.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:14.619
And you have another piece, the

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.569
number of playable guitars

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:18.039
in the world, which has increased

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.959
massively. So that speaks

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.339
to the idea that it's a pretty good

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.229
world where a lot of

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.539
people are expressing themselves

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:26.889
creatively.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.019
That's an indicator that we're doing

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:29.469
pretty well.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:31.329
Yes. The numbers of the guitars

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.369
is absolutely

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.259
astonishing because it went

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.509
from a couple of hundreds of

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.459
decades ago to tens

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:40.939
of thousands now.

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:42.789
So, I mean, the increase

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:43.779
is just incredible.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.159
Going in the other direction, you

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.199
have a painting that talks about

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.179
the decline in the number of deaths

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:51.249
from natural disasters,

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.229
which is also something that,

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.179
again, if you're I would say

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:57.009
if you're over 40, you should have

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:59.049
no problem realizing like when

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:00.879
you were younger, you would hear

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.379
all of these stories about, you

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:03.459
know, hundreds of thousands of

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:05.289
people starving to death or

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:07.239
dying because of earthquakes

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.399
or volcanic eruptions or tidal

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:10.959
waves. And you don't really hear

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:12.039
that very much anymore.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.199
But we think the world

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:15.669
is getting worse on that scale.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.559
Yes. Well, it's exactly

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.819
what we talked about in the

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:20.949
beginning. It's that there has never

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.829
been a news report about

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:25.899
there was no earthquake

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.519
in California today.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.799
Right. So all the positive

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:31.749
things just go unreported.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:33.699
But even the absolute number

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:35.798
of people dying went down.

00:10:35.799 --> 00:10:37.419
It is a fraction of what it used to

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:39.099
be. And that's certainly true of

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:40.809
things like pandemics and what not.

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:43.239
So however bad COVID was,

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:44.799
the response was quicker and more

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.139
effective than the 1918

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:47.959
flu epidemic.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:50.679
Yes, I always found it fascinating

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:52.539
that during COVID one of

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:54.399
the words that was

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.559
used the most in the media, also

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:57.639
in the New York Times was

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:58.749
"unprecedented.".

00:10:58.750 --> 00:10:59.199
Right.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:01.209
Even though the people

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:03.579
who wrote that nearly

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:05.499
had the knowledge that this

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.449
is not unprecedented, maybe

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:09.459
unprecedented this year, but for

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:10.989
sure, not unprecedented this

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:11.829
century, Right.

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.779
Where you had AIDS that killed

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:15.639
30 million people, you had the

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.829
Spanish flu was between

00:11:17.830 --> 00:11:19.719
50 and 100 million people and so

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:19.929
on.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:21.609
You know, a number of the pieces

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.139
talk about immigrants and

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.819
immigration being a positive thing.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:26.739
You yourself are an immigrant from

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.569
Austria to New York.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:29.979
You first came here in the late

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:31.809
eighties. You settled here

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:33.549
fully in the early nineties.

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:35.679
What drew you to New York and

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:38.019
how does that reflect

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:39.159
in your work?

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:41.259
Well, I came here first right

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:42.309
after high school.

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:44.409
It was a high school gift

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:45.699
from my brother in law.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:47.079
It was in 81.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.179
So New York was, from its

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.189
now perspective, in

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.559
a pretty bad shape.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.419
I would say crime was incredibly

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.249
high, but it was

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:58.539
just fascinating to me.

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:00.129
Even though I was born in a very

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:02.289
pretty small town in Austria,

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.269
I was always attracted by

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:05.619
big places.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.019
I think it's also a lovely

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:10.059
mix of American

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.979
friendliness, which might

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:14.259
be odd because, you know,

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:15.260
New York is not known as a friendly
city.

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:17.469
Exactly.

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:19.299
But ultimately it is

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.229
quite open.

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:22.059
And I haven't done a piece

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:24.249
of it yet, but I've just

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.199
come across and I checked it and

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:27.489
it seems to hold up.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.349
I just came across data

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:32.049
that showed that refugees,

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:34.479
not immigrants, refugees

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:36.369
that have been in this country for

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:38.769
25 years or longer

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:40.749
have a 30% higher

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:43.289
income than the average American.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.369
And with immigrants, okay, I believe

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:47.309
it. But the refugees,

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:49.049
it's pretty astonishing that

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.779
refugees in their lifetime,

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:53.189
not in the beginning when they come

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.169
here, but in their lifetime

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.059
would surpass

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:58.379
the locals.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.899
I find that to be the strongest

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:02.939
indicator that there is

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:04.799
an American dream, that that is

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.389
a life that I've come across.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:08.849
And I know it has been incredibly

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.559
fashionable to say that the American

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:11.699
dream is dead.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.589
And even I feel

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:15.419
sort of like silly sitting

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:17.729
here are claiming otherwise.

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:19.379
But those numbers point in that

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:20.399
direction.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.079
Your background is as a graphic

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:22.529
designer.

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:23.249
Yes.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:25.319
Which is more functional than

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.219
you know, than art.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:27.220
Right.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:28.472
True. 100%.

00:13:28.473 --> 00:13:30.299
Do you see a sharp distinction

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.069
that many artists or many critics

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:33.839
would draw between kind of

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.879
commercial art or instrumental

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.979
art and real art?

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.419
Is that a false dichotomy?

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:42.309
I can argue it both ways.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.319
If people ask me, "What do you

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:46.179
call yourself?" I definitely am a

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:48.369
designer and the work that I'm doing

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.199
here, I would also see as

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.119
design work

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:53.949
simply because

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:55.509
I would say that there is a

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.489
functionality to it

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:00.039
and which I find pleasing.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.259
But I also totally understand

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:04.479
that many artists would

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.849
not want any functionality

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:07.839
to their pieces.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:09.249
That's the whole point of art,

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.169
right? It's that it resists

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:13.209
being used for some other purpose.

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:14.889
And I would say even there would

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:16.779
have to really say it's the

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:18.699
whole point of contemporary

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:20.559
art, because art

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.149
by itself, if you look at, has

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:24.129
historically, of course, also had

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:25.359
many functions.

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:27.309
Many of the most

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.139
famous artists in

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:31.269
the past did very functional

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:33.009
work. You know, Michelangelo

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.409
designed candlestick holders.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.449
Leonardo obviously did

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:38.919
many, many other things.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:40.839
There is no such thing as

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:42.499
pure functionality.

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:44.019
It's all on a scale.

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:45.849
Talk a little bit about your process

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:48.309
for the pieces in this exhibition.

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:50.319
Basically, you buy cheap

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:52.209
paintings from the 18th

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:54.549
and 19th centuries and then

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:56.499
you put data visualizations

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:57.099
over them.

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.319
It originally started with pieces

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:01.209
that we still had in the

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:02.799
attic, in the house that I grew up

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:04.869
with that came from my great

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:06.669
great grandfather's store, who was

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:08.469
an antique dealer in western

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:10.359
Austria. And the stuff that didn't

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:12.429
sell wound up still being

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:14.349
stored still to this very day.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:15.939
So the first pieces really came from

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:18.339
there. And since then,

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:20.469
I'm by now, I think

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:22.449
connected to about

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:24.369
through the auction houses,

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:26.049
all of them in Central Europe,

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:27.759
mostly Austria.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:29.499
So what do you show money or what do

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:30.969
you pay for a piece that you're

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.379
going to paint over?

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:34.719
I think they're all

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:38.499
for sure under €4,000,

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:40.389
which, you know, I'm kind of a

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:42.609
beneficiary of the fact

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:44.769
that specifically 19th

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:46.809
century art, but also 18th century

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:48.659
art is very much out

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.049
of the fashion right now.

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:52.299
What is the pleasure of painting

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.189
over, you know, a piece from the

00:15:54.190 --> 00:15:56.049
early 19th century

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:57.939
with a data visualization that

00:15:57.940 --> 00:15:59.619
shows how much better things have

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:00.339
gotten?

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:01.779
Well, we're not really painting

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:04.209
over. We're really basically

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:06.189
taking the piece apart.

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:08.229
We are plucking holes into

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.809
it and we are inserting

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.129
the new data.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.109
So the piece, if you want,

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:15.999
is destroyed, which

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.979
I had given some

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.589
thoughts about meaning, ultimately,

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:22.689
I solved it for myself, thinking

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:24.579
that if somebody in

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:26.529
200 years takes my

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:28.569
work that they

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:30.789
found at tiny auction

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:32.889
houses and makes a new piece

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:34.419
out of it, I think I would be very

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:35.199
happy about it.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:37.059
But the process really is

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:39.099
I buy these pieces at auction.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.139
By then I've already had

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:42.969
received the photo, so

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.969
I ultimately try out designs

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:48.519
and these can be inspired

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:49.689
by the data.

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:51.549
They can be inspired by a color

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:53.679
scheme, they can be inspired

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.899
by form that can be inspired

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:57.039
by a composition.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:59.109
They can be a reaction

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:01.449
on the existing piece.

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:03.429
But ultimately, by and

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:05.469
large, I'm trying

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.739
to go against the composition

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.868
that is existing because

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:13.838
that sort of seems to create

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:16.179
the kind of tension

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:17.589
that ultimately.

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:22.249
Makes a good new composition.

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.899
How much did you increase on

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:25.789
average? Just make it up if you have

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.009
to. You know, if the average

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:30.259
painting that you started with

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:32.089
cost €4,000,

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:33.469
what was the average that you were

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:34.249
selling it for?

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:35.929
How much did you increase the value?

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:37.789
I think it's probably times

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:39.169
two or three.

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:40.459
But of course, there's also

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:42.889
significant cost involved

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:44.569
in creating this.

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:45.559
You know, like there is an

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.389
incredible amount of

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:49.429
just hours that go into that, not

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:52.249
just myself or for my own, but

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.229
there's a whole team working on this

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:55.939
in the Brooklyn Navy Yard, there are

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:58.209
costs in renting the space,

00:17:58.210 --> 00:17:58.999
all of that stuff.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.259
So there is that.

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:02.629
But ultimately, I

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:04.639
actually try to keep

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:06.619
the price as low

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:08.479
as it's possible that it

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:10.059
still makes sense.

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:11.929
Yeah, I want this to be in

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:13.909
as many homes as possible.

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:15.439
That's the functionality of it,

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:17.629
because ultimately the goal and

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:19.489
in therefore really

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:22.069
it's embedded in the concept

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:24.229
that these things hang on somebody's

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:26.449
wall as a reminder

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:28.219
that what they just read on Twitter

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:29.539
doesn't mean it's the end of the

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:30.049
world.

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:32.719
You've worked with a number of very,

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:34.849
you know, globally known recording

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:36.589
artists like Lou Reed, like the

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:37.909
Talking Heads, like the Rolling

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:38.719
Stones.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.609
And you did an album cover for The

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:42.169
Rolling Stones. Which album was that

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:42.409
again?

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:43.339
Bridges To Babylon.

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:45.109
Yeah. Yeah. So, you know, that's

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:46.339
like a platinum record.

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:48.679
What what does it feel like to

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.539
know that your images are going

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:52.759
out to, you know, a million people

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:53.509
or more?

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:54.829
Fantastic. You know, it's also

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:56.749
something that I love about design

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:59.149
is that it's a pretty

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:00.979
democratic way

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:02.809
of working where you know,

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:04.939
we did a Jay-Z cover where the first

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:07.129
one was 5 million for

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:08.130
the price of $20-25,

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:11.839
which I don't think there is

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.449
a publication in the art world

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:16.699
that reaches those kind of numbers.

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:18.739
I very much love the fact

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:20.959
that design tends to live

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:22.669
within our lives.

00:19:22.670 --> 00:19:24.319
I see myself as a communication

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:25.939
designer more so than a graphic

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:27.619
designer. I think the challenge

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:29.659
specifically for somebody like

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:31.609
me is to make these

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:32.610
things.

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:34.609
Interesting enough.

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:38.189
That even though they're positive,

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:40.199
people will be engaged.

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.059
And, you know, that's the challenge.

